<title>USA: Oklahoma rains slow row crop harvest.</title>
<headline>Oklahoma rains slow row crop harvest.</headline>
<dateline>OKLAHOMA CITY 1996-11-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>Harvesting of row crops was slowed in many areas by rains during the week ended Sunday, according to the weekly state crop report.</p>
<p>Pasture condition fell slightly as cooler temperatures slowed forage growth.</p>
<p>Wheat planted was reported at 98 percent, compared to 94 percent a week ago, 96 percent a year ago and 95 percent on a five-year average.</p>
<p>Wheat emerged 85 percent vs 72 w/a, 66 y/a, 73 avg.</p>
<p>Wheat rated 81 percent good to excellent, vs 75 at that level last week.</p>
<p>Sorghum harvested 34 percent vs 29 w/a, 43 y/a, 46 avg.</p>
<p>Sorghum rated 92 percent good/excellent, vs 91 last week.</p>
<p>Soybeans harvested 62 percent vs 55 w/a, 57 y/a, 55 avg.</p>
<p>Soybeans mature 93 percent vs 89 w/a, 79 y/a, 86 avg.</p>
<p>Cotton bolls opening 84 percent vs 79 w/a, 76 y/a, 85 avg.</p>
<p>Cotton harvested 24 percent vs 19 w/a, 14 y/a, 32 avg.</p>
<p>Cotton rated 33 percent good/excellent, vs 33 last week.</p>
<p>Pasture and range condition rated 73 percent good to excellent vs 77 last week.</p>
<p>--Kansas City bureau, 816 561-8671</p>
</text>
